AHS-016 - Communication Model

Document ID: AHS-016
Title: Communication Model
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

Architectural Views: Process, Logical, Implementation

1. Purpose

This specification defines the canonical Communication Model of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Communication Model establishes how Atlas components, services, users, and external systems exchange engineering artifacts while preserving semantic integrity, reliability, traceability, and implementation independence.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Communication patterns
Message semantics
Communication channels
Delivery guarantees
Reliability
Communication lifecycle
Error handling

This specification does not define network protocols, transport mechanisms, or vendor-specific messaging technologies.

3. Canonical Definition

Communication is the governed exchange of canonical engineering artifacts between Atlas participants through standardized interaction patterns that preserve engineering meaning, integrity, and traceability.

4. Communication Objectives

Atlas communication SHALL:

Preserve engineering semantics.
Preserve Canonical Engineering Artifacts (CEA).
Support synchronous and asynchronous interactions.
Ensure reliable delivery where required.
Maintain traceability.
Support scalable distributed architectures.
Remain technology independent.
5. Communication Principles

Every communication SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Semantic Preservation

Communication SHALL NOT alter engineering meaning.

Canonical Artifacts

Communication SHALL exchange Canonical Engineering Artifacts.

Loose Coupling

Participants SHALL communicate through contracts rather than implementation details.

Reliability

Communication SHALL provide defined delivery guarantees.

Traceability

Every exchange SHALL be traceable.

Observability

Communication SHALL expose sufficient operational information for monitoring and diagnostics.

6. Communication Participants

Atlas recognizes the following communication participants.

Cognitive Services
Governance Services
Platform Services
Integration Services
Human Interfaces
External Engineering Systems
Autonomous Agents

Every participant SHALL communicate through canonical interfaces.

7. Communication Patterns

Atlas supports six canonical communication patterns.

7.1 Request–Response

A consumer requests a capability and waits for a response.

Suitable for:

Engineering queries
Decision requests
Validation services
Consumer
    │ Request
    ▼
Provider
    │ Response
    ▼
Consumer
7.2 Event-Driven

Execution occurs when an engineering event is published.

Examples:

CAD model updated
Sensor reading received
Constraint modified
Producer
    │
Publish Event
    │
 ┌──┴─────┐
 ▼        ▼
Service A Service B
7.3 Publish–Subscribe

Multiple participants subscribe to engineering events.

Subscribers remain independent.

Publisher
      │
      ▼
 Event Bus
 ┌────┼────┐
 ▼    ▼    ▼
S1   S2   S3
7.4 Streaming

Continuous flow of engineering information.

Examples:

IoT telemetry
Digital Twin updates
Structural monitoring
Construction progress feeds
7.5 Pipeline

Information progresses through ordered cognitive operations.

Observation

↓

Evidence

↓

Understanding

↓

Reasoning
7.6 Broadcast

One participant communicates with all interested participants.

Typical uses:

Policy updates
Configuration changes
Emergency notifications
8. Communication Channels

Atlas communication MAY occur over:

Internal service channels
External integration channels
Human interaction channels
Administrative channels
Monitoring channels

Channels SHALL preserve communication semantics regardless of implementation.

9. Message Model

Every communication SHALL include:

Element	Description
Message ID	Globally unique identifier
Sender	Originating participant
Receiver	Intended participant(s)
Timestamp	Time of transmission
Message Type	Request, Response, Event, Notification
Payload	Canonical Engineering Artifact
Context	Engineering context
Version	Communication version
Traceability Reference	Links to source artifacts
10. Delivery Guarantees

Atlas recognizes four delivery guarantees.

Best Effort

Delivery attempted without guarantee.

At Least Once

Delivery guaranteed but duplicates MAY occur.

At Most Once

Delivery attempted once with no duplicates.

Exactly Once

Delivery guaranteed exactly once where supported by the implementation.

The required guarantee SHALL be explicitly documented for each communication contract.

